hroughout there's many steps and  chip and there's many places that need  optimization for whatever your  experiment requires whether it's out do  to the cell line or the type of target  there's lots of places that require our  optimization and anywhere along the way  that you can check to see if it's  working I highly recommend that so  things like checking to confirm that  your fragmentation is good and checking  to confirm your life is good in checking  that your qPCR primers are good are  always to just ensure along the way that  you're getting the best experiment and  with that I'd like to thank a number of  my colleagues at Thermo Fisher  Scientific who helped put this webinar  together as well as providing data for  this webinar additionally I had several  collaborators kindly give me data to  include in this webinar and I thank them  very much for that and lastly I think  epi cipher who shared um not only data  provided from their own work but also  have been great collaborators along the  way  and with that I thank you and I will  take questions  Thank You dr. small for your informative  presentation we will now start the live  Q&amp;A portion of the webinar if you have a  question you'd like to ask please do so  now just click on the answer question  box located on the far left your screen  and we'll answer as many of your  questions as we have time for so let's  get started our first question is chip  western seem to show that the antibody  is pulling down the protein of interest  but how does it show specificities  that's a great point because what I  showed you is that you can see that your  protein is being pulled down but you  have no idea what else is being pulled  down and the case of the histone  modification it gave a little bit more  information because the peptide  competition was at it so that you could  see then that it was competed off only  when the tri methyl peptide was at it  but you're absolutely correct that that  that doesn't fully show specificity so  things I would recommend doing our upon  do staining of your membrane or silver  staining of your gel and what you would  expect to see is mostly just your  protein you may see some other proteins  especially if your protein you know is  in a complex but that's going to add  another layer of specificity by being  able to just see a pretty clean gel or  membrane where you're not seeing that  protein  now how do I to excuse me now how do I  identify where the nonspecific binding  is coming from and therefore how to  troubleshoot it well yeah that's a hard  question because probably saw throughout  there's many reasons so the first thing  is to confirm that your antibody is  really specific and if you if you're  confident that your antibody is specific  then that it's not coming directly from  your antibody then there are definitely  things you can do unfortunately if  you're not confident your antibody  specific or you find your antibodies not  specific then you just need to start  over but if you feel that your antibody  is good one place to look at is  chromatin fragmentation if you want to  make sure again that you're on that kind  of 200 400 maybe a little above base  pairs because a lack of good chromatin  fragmentation you basically not only is  your efficiency of pull down lower but  you really kind of increase how much you  can amplifying your DNA of where the  protein is so that footprint becomes  much bigger and then you're not getting  that same nice resolution so it might be  it might be that's not pulling down  something nonspecific that it but that  it's more that you're only seeing your  you're seeing kind of these large  fragments that you're amplifying another  thing would be I mean I'd highly  recommend also using methanol free  formaldehyde because um methanol  contamination can cause problems with  fragmentation another thing that can  cause problems with fragmentation is  over cross-linking so if you've  cross-linked for too long